            <ArticleTitle>Atrial tachycardia treated by coil embolization of a giant coronary artery fistula.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>651.e1-4</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.repc.2014.05.006</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0870-2551(14)00229-7</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Coronary artery fistulas are the second most frequently seen coronary anomaly following abnormalities of coronary artery origin and distribution. A coronary fistula is defined as a direct communication between a coronary artery and any cardiac chamber or vessel. Treatment options include percutaneous embolization and surgical intervention. Herein, we present a case of a giant coronary artery fistula and right atrial tachycardia that was induced during a diagnostic electrophysiologic study but was not inducible after the successful treatment of the fistula. This is the first case indicating this association.</AbstractText>
